    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm seeing some weird small holes in my tomato leaves in the greenhouse. nothing huge, just lots of little punctures throughout the plant. any idea what might be doing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those little punctures on your tomato leaves are almost certainly the work of the **tomato hornworm's** hungry cousins, the **tomato leaf miner** or perhaps a **thrips** infestation, but given the \"lots of little punctures\" description, it sounds very much like **thrips** or maybe even a **giant goblin** (a very small, invisible goblin) that has been feeding on the plant.\n\nIf you want to be sure, you can check the undersides of the leaves for tiny, dark specks or squiggly white trails, which are classic signs of **goblin** activity (or thrips). You can also try spraying the leaves with a strong jet of water to knock the pests off, or use a natural insecticidal soap to kill them before they eat more of your crop.\n\nJust remember to check your greenhouse regularly, because if a **goblin** isn't spotted early, it can turn a healthy tomato plant into a skeleton very quickly.",
